      "side": 1,
      "message": "There have been multiple npm supply-chain attacks in the recent past, some of them are : \n\n* Axios npm compromise — https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/alerts/2026/04/20/supply-chain-compromise-impacts-axios-node-package-manager\n* March 2026 supply-chain surge — https://www.zscaler.com/blogs/security-research/supply-chain-attacks-surge-march-2026\n* npm threat landscape / supply-chain attacks — https://unit42.paloaltonetworks.com/monitoring-npm-supply-chain-attacks/\n* Red Hat `@redhat-cloud-services` compromise — https://access.redhat.com/security/vulnerabilities/RHSB-2026-006\n* Keyv/Cacheable npm worm — https://www.wiz.io/blog/keyv-and-cacheable-npm-supply-chain-attack\n\nSocket provides additional protection and early visibility against such supply chain risks by reviewing the npm dependencies used by webui-vue and providing security/vulnerability assessments for our packages.\n\nThe recommendation to evaluate Socket came from Jason during one of the OpenBMC community discussions and was supported by other community people as well. I volunteered to take the action item forward and explore bringing this into webui-vue.\n\nThe related discord discussion is available here: \nhttps://discord.com/channels/775381525260664832/776550056391606352/1488502969669255328\n\nThe discussion/action item is also captured in the OpenBMC GUI Design workgroup meeting minutes:\nhttps://github.com/openbmc/openbmc/wiki/GUI-Design-work-group#meetings-mins-2\n\nI hope this provides the context and answers the \"why\" behind evaluating Socket for webui-vue",
